(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 70-year-old man with a recent transient ischemic attack has a high-grade carotid stenosis on duplex and is being prepared for intervention. Which study is considered the gold standard?","choices":["Magnetic resonance angiography","Carotid auscultation for a bruit","Digital subtraction angiography","Duplex ultrasonography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digital subtraction angiography is an invasive angiographic technique considered the gold standard for carotid artery disease. It is not used for screening but for guiding therapeutic interventions, which fits a man whose high-grade stenosis after a transient ischemic attack is already headed for intervention. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who takes no anticoagulant develops sudden, severe pain in her left leg two hours ago. The leg is cold and pale, pulses are absent below the femoral artery, and she has numbness over the dorsum of the foot. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Critical limb ischemia","Atheroembolism with blue toes","Intermittent claudication","Acute embolic limb ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sudden severe pain with a cold, pale leg and absent pulses below the femoral artery is acute limb ischemia, sudden occlusion of a previously patent artery. Thromboemboli are most common and the heart is the source in 80 to 90% of cases, with atrial fibrillation a named source; numbness over the dorsum of the foot is an early neurologic sign. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman who smokes has cramping in her right calf after walking one block that eases after she stands still for several minutes. Her pedal pulses are diminished on the right. Which is the best initial test?","choices":["Magnetic resonance angiography","Ankle-brachial index","Conventional angiography","Computed tomography angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calf cramping after one block that eases with standing still, plus diminished right pedal pulses in a smoker, is classic claudication. The ankle-brachial index is a simple bedside test and the important initial test, with 0.4 to 0.9 the claudication band; it divides the higher ankle pressure by the higher arm pressure. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has had six days of worsening left leg pain and coolness. The limb has no sensory loss or weakness, and imaging shows an arterial thrombus. Which intervention fits this time frame?","choices":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Major amputation","Oral aspirin alone","Supervised exercise program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her ischemia has lasted six days, well within the indication for intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis, which is acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration, with alteplase as the thrombolytic drug. With no sensory loss or weakness, her limb is far from the irreversible category that requires amputation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who smoked for 40 years has no symptoms, but a widened, pulsatile aorta is felt on routine abdominal examination. Which is the study of choice for initial screening for an aneurysm?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Abdominal ultrasound","Abdominal X-ray"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A widened, pulsatile aorta found on routine examination of an asymptomatic 68-year-old smoker calls for screening, and abdominal ultrasound is the diagnostic study of choice for initial screening for an aneurysm. It is also used for serial documentation of aneurysm size. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has had progressive ischemic pain in his right leg for three weeks and is found to have an arterial occlusion. Which intervention falls outside its indication for him?","choices":["Arterial bypass procedure","Intravenous heparin","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Surgical thromboembolectomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis, using alteplase, is indicated only for acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration. His ischemia has progressed for three weeks, about 21 days, which puts him outside that window, while heparin, thromboembolectomy, bypass and amputation carry no such limit. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 66, 67"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with no neurologic symptoms has a 90% carotid stenosis on duplex. He has advanced cancer, and his expected survival is about two years. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Medical management","Carotid endarterectomy","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Endarterectomy after one more duplex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Surgery for asymptomatic stenosis is advised only above 80% together with a life expectancy over five years. His 90% stenosis meets the first criterion, but advanced cancer limits survival to about two years, so he is managed medically with antiplatelet therapy, comorbidity control and smoking cessation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man who smokes has a 4.3 cm asymptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysm and a blood pressure of 158/94. He asks what he can do to help himself. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Quit smoking; control blood pressure","Blood pressure matters only after surgery","Smoking matters only for leg disease","Nothing changes until repair"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of a thoracic aortic aneurysm includes smoking cessation, beta-adrenergic blockers and aggressive blood pressure control, which target his smoking and his pressure of 158/94. At 4.3 cm, under 4.5 cm, he also needs surveillance with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging every 3 to 5 years. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man collapses with sudden severe back pain. He is pale, his blood pressure is 78/50, and a pulsatile abdominal mass is felt. There is a Grey Turner sign (flank ecchymosis). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Large-bore access and emergency surgery","Abdominal ultrasound surveillance","Admit for pain control and serial exams","Beta blocker, then elective repair"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sudden severe back pain, a pulsatile abdominal mass and hypotension of 78/50, with a Grey Turner sign of retroperitoneal hemorrhage, mean a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm. He is hemodynamically unstable and this is a surgical emergency: large-bore intravenous catheters, type and crossmatch, and immediate surgery. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has a painful, swollen right groin two days after a cardiac catheterization through the femoral artery. Ultrasound shows a blood-filled cavity outside the arterial wall, communicating with the lumen. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Femoral true aneurysm","Acute limb ischemia","Atheroembolism","Femoral pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm is a communication between the arterial lumen and overlying connective tissue after arterial rupture, forming a blood-filled cavity outside the vessel wall. The common site is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, from laceration by the access needle, and pain comes from swelling or nerve compression. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with known aortic atherosclerosis has sudden pain and tenderness of two toes, which have turned blue. Which pulse finding is expected on examination?","choices":["A pulse with a double systolic peak","Distal pulses usually remain palpable","Pulses absent below the femoral artery","Pulses absent in both groins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden painful blue toes with known aortic atherosclerosis point to atheroembolism. The debris of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol tends to lodge in small vessels of muscle and skin and may not occlude large vessels, so distal pulses usually remain palpable. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man had 20 minutes of weakness and numbness in his right hand four months ago that fully resolved. Duplex ultrasonography shows 75% stenosis of the left internal carotid artery. A friend with no symptoms was told surgery waits until stenosis passes 80%, and he asks whether the same applies to him. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Yes; surgery waits until over 80%","No; he needs medical therapy only","Yes; resolved symptoms do not count","No; over 70% already qualifies him"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His 20 minutes of hand weakness and numbness was a transient ischemic attack (a focal loss of neurologic function lasting under 24 hours) within the last six months, so he is symptomatic. Surgery is advised for symptomatic stenosis over 70%, which his 75% meets; the over-80% rule is for asymptomatic patients. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 72, 75"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with ischemic rest pain notices that walking around the room eases his foot pain, although walking used to cause calf pain. Which explanation should he be given?","choices":["Walking releases natural pain relief","Walking drains venous blood away","Walking lowers his blood pressure","Dependency uses gravity to aid flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain is relieved by hanging the leg over the edge of the bed or by walking around. In contrast to claudication, walking around uses the gravity effect of dependence on extremity perfusion, which is why walking that once caused his calf pain now eases his foot. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with stable calf claudication is starting exercise therapy. She asks whether walking on her own at home is as good as a formal program. Which is the most appropriate recommendation?","choices":["Refer to supervised exercise rehabilitation","Walk at home; supervision adds nothing","Refer for bypass graft surgery now","Avoid exercise until pain resolves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She should be referred to an exercise rehabilitation program, because a supervised program is more successful than a home-based program. Consistent moderate exercise, at least 30 to 45 minutes three times per week, improves walking distance in stable claudication. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has claudication after half a block despite six months of supervised exercise, smoking cessation and cilostazol, and can no longer work. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start intravenous heparin","Proceed to amputation","Refer for revascularization","Repeat the exercise program"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Revascularization is recommended for claudication with severe functional disability and an inadequate response to exercise or drug therapy, which fits half-block claudication despite six months of supervised exercise, smoking cessation and cilostazol. Endovascular options include angioplasty with or without stenting and atherectomy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has had painful blue toes for several days after atheroemboli from proximal plaques. Which complication may follow in the affected digits?","choices":["Rupture of the popliteal artery","Hoarseness from nerve stretch","Heart block from nodal fibrosis","Digital necrosis and gangrene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her painful blue toes after atheroemboli reflect digital vascular occlusion causing ischemia, the blue toe syndrome. Digital necrosis and gangrene may develop, and localized tenderness, pallor and livedo reticularis occur at the sites of emboli. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has an asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm that now measures 5.9 cm. Repair is planned. Which approach is usually required for this location?","choices":["Watch until it reaches 6.5 cm","Open repair with root replacement","Endovascular repair only","Surveillance every 3 to 5 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgical repair is generally indicated for ascending aneurysms over 5.5 cm, and hers is 5.9 cm. An ascending thoracic aneurysm often requires an open surgical approach with aortic root replacement, whereas a descending one can be repaired open or endovascularly. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has sudden coolness of her left foot with minimal pain in the toes only. She has no sensory loss and no muscle weakness. Which prognosis applies to this limb?","choices":["Salvageable if promptly treated","Full recovery without treatment","Salvageable only with immediate revascularization","Major tissue loss is inevitable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sudden coolness with minimal pain in the toes only, no sensory loss and no muscle weakness, describes a marginally threatened limb. Its prognosis is salvageable if treated promptly, and there is sufficient time for vascular imaging such as computed tomography angiography. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 59, 62, 64"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has a 1.4 cm femoral pseudoaneurysm on ultrasound after cardiac catheterization. She is comfortable and the limb is well perfused. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Watch and wait","Open surgical repair","Intravenous heparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm under 2 cm in diameter may undergo spontaneous thrombosis and regression, and hers is 1.4 cm in a comfortable patient with a well-perfused limb. Watchful waiting is appropriate; ultrasound-guided thrombin injection is the treatment when a leak needs sealing. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension has an acute aortic dissection with severe tearing chest pain and a blood pressure of 176/100. Which intravenous medication should be started to lower her pressure?","choices":["Esmolol","Alteplase","Heparin","Cilostazol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her acute dissection with a pressure of 176/100 needs progression stopped by reducing blood pressure, with a systolic target of 100 to 120 mmHg. The drugs named are a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside, given with pain management before surgical grafting. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 51, 66, 67, 118"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man had a transient ischemic attack last month. Duplex shows 85% stenosis of the ipsilateral internal carotid artery, and he is a good surgical candidate. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Antiplatelet therapy alone","Carotid stenting via the groin","Repeat duplex in one year","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A transient ischemic attack last month makes his disease symptomatic, and surgery is advised for symptomatic stenosis over 70%; his is 85%. Because he is a good surgical candidate, carotid endarterectomy, removal of plaque through a surgical incision, is the gold standard for high-grade stenosis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is newly diagnosed with peripheral artery disease after an abnormal ankle-brachial index. He has no chest symptoms. Because half of such patients share it, which associated condition should be evaluated next?","choices":["Pseudoaneurysm of the femoral artery","Venous insufficiency","Coronary artery disease","Aortic dissection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral artery disease shares the pathophysiology of coronary artery disease, and 50% of patients with peripheral artery disease also have coronary artery disease. Even without chest symptoms, his new diagnosis should prompt evaluation of the coronary circulation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man's asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measured 4.1 cm six months ago and now measures 4.8 cm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Wait until it reaches 5.5 cm","Repair the aneurysm","Ultrasound in three years","Recheck in 6 to 12 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Evidence of rapid growth, 0.5 cm in six months, means the aneurysm should be repaired. His grew from 4.1 to 4.8 cm, a 0.7 cm increase in six months, so repair is indicated even though 4.8 cm is below the size thresholds of 5.0 cm in women and over 5.5 cm in men. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 4.2 cm, unchanged from last year. Which follow-up is most appropriate?","choices":["Elective repair now","Emergency repair today","Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Ultrasound every three years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surveillance frequency depends on size: an aneurysm of 4.0 to 5.4 cm is imaged with ultrasound or computed tomography every 6 to 12 months. His 4.2 cm aneurysm is asymptomatic and unchanged from last year, so it has no growth trigger for repair. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with a 6.1 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm is scheduled for elective repair. He asks how the surgeon will choose between endovascular and open repair. Which factor guides the choice?","choices":["His age and comorbidities","The color of his skin","The result of his ankle index","Whether he has a carotid bruit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two repair methods exist: endovascular aneurysm repair, a modular graft placed through the iliac or femoral arteries to line the aorta and exclude the sac, and open replacement with a tube or bifurcated prosthetic graft. The choice considers the patient's age and medical comorbidities. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is diagnosed with an acute aortic dissection. His family asks what happens if it is not treated. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Only the legs are at risk","Untreated, mortality is high","It usually heals by itself","It turns into a pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute untreated aortic dissection has a high mortality rate. That is why management moves quickly: systolic pressure is reduced to 100 to 120 mmHg with esmolol or nitroprusside, pain is managed, and surgery grafts and replaces the diseased portion of the aorta. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has sudden, severe, tearing chest pain radiating to his back. Blood pressure is 184/106. Imaging confirms an aortic dissection. Which blood pressure goal applies?","choices":["Systolic above 140 mmHg","Systolic below 80 mmHg","No change until surgery","Systolic 100 to 120 mmHg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stopping progression of a dissection requires reducing blood pressure, with a systolic goal of 100 to 120 mmHg, using a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. His pressure of 184/106 is far above that range, and pain management and surgical grafting follow. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a 4.8 cm descending thoracic aortic aneurysm, and he now has back pain that is attributed to the aneurysm. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Repair only above 6.0 cm","Repair the aneurysm","Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Imaging every 3 to 5 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Repair is recommended for all symptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysms. His 4.8 cm descending aneurysm is below the asymptomatic threshold of 6.0 cm, but back pain attributed to the aneurysm makes it symptomatic, and that alone indicates repair. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has lifestyle-limiting calf claudication despite six months of supervised exercise and antiplatelet therapy, and revascularization is being planned. Which study is the gold standard for defining his arterial anatomy?","choices":["Ankle-brachial index","Pulse volume recording","Conventional angiography","Duplex ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Conventional angiography is the gold standard and the most accurate and informative test. It is invasive, requiring vascular puncture with catheters placed at the stenosis and contrast injected, and it is used primarily for guidance when an intervention, like his planned revascularization, is planned. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man is scheduled for a femoral-popliteal bypass graft using his saphenous vein. He asks what can go wrong. Which complications should he be counseled about?","choices":["Kidney stones and gout flares","Heart attack, stroke, graft infection","Hoarseness and difficulty swallowing","Heart block needing a pacemaker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bypass graft surgery, here a femoral-popliteal bypass with his saphenous vein, is invasive and highly effective, but its complications include myocardial infarction and stroke, infection of the graft and peripheral embolization. These are what he should be counseled about. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
